8.9.m:
Then the rivers and the oceans, bringing jewels from all around, / all approached the king for his royal consecration.
The venerable grandfather [Brahmā], accompanied by seers and gods, / and all beings, both stationary and moving,
assembling then, consecrated the king, the son of Vena. / Consecrated by the gods headed by Aṅgiras,
the illustrious and glorious Pṛthu, son of Vena, attained supreme sovereignty. / His subjects, not pleased by his father, were delighted by Vena's son;
hence his name "Rājan" (king) arose from their affection (anurāga). / When he marched towards the ocean, the waters stood still;
mountains broke apart, and his banner met no obstruction. / The earth yielded crops without being tilled, and foods were produced simply by thought;
cows yielded all desires, and there was honey in every leaf-cup.
At that very time, the Māgadha was born again, / while the Sāman-singers were chanting, from the ladle-vessel of the All-gods' rite.
